---
/**
 * ============================================================================
 *  DO NOT ADD ANALYTICS, ADS, OR THIRD-PARTY SCRIPTS TO THIS PAGE.
 * ============================================================================
 *  Same commitment as the other tools: no ads, no trackers, works offline.
 *  Any external script makes those published claims false.
 * ============================================================================
 */
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { Navbar } from '../../components/Navbar';
import { K8sQosCalculator } from '../../components/tools/K8sQosCalculator';

const faqs = [
  {
    "q": "What are the three QoS classes?",
    "a": "Guaranteed: every container sets requests and limits for both CPU and memory, with request equal to limit. BestEffort: nothing is set at all. Burstable: everything in between. One container falling short of the Guaranteed rules makes the whole pod Burstable."
  },
  {
    "q": "What is a QoS class in Kubernetes?",
    "a": "A label the kubelet assigns to every pod at admission time, based purely on the requests and limits its containers declare. You never set it directly; it is derived. Its job is to rank pods for eviction when a node runs short of resources, so it decides which workloads are sacrificed first when the node is under pressure."
  },
  {
    "q": "How does Kubernetes decide the QoS class?",
    "a": "Three rules, checked in order. If every container in the pod sets both CPU and memory requests and limits, and each request equals its limit, the pod is Guaranteed. If no container sets any request or limit at all, it is BestEffort. Everything else is Burstable. A single container missing a single value is enough to drop the whole pod from Guaranteed to Burstable, which is the rule that surprises people most."
  },
  {
    "q": "Why does the QoS class matter?",
    "a": "It sets the eviction order when a node runs short of memory: BestEffort pods go first, then Burstable pods exceeding their requests, and Guaranteed pods last. For anything stateful or latency-critical, the class is effectively your seat on the lifeboat."
  },
  {
    "q": "Should everything be Guaranteed then?",
    "a": "No. Guaranteed reserves the full limit for every pod, which forfeits the overcommit that makes clusters efficient. A common pattern is Guaranteed for critical stateful services, Burstable with honest requests for most workloads, and BestEffort only for genuinely disposable jobs."
  },
  {
    "q": "What happens when a container exceeds its memory limit?",
    "a": "It is OOM-killed by the kernel, regardless of QoS class. Exceeding the CPU limit merely throttles. That asymmetry is why the calculator flags missing memory limits: without one, a leak grows until the node itself comes under pressure and the eviction order decides who pays."
  },
  {
    "q": "Is my configuration sent anywhere?",
    "a": "No. Classification happens in your browser and the page works offline."
  }
];

const pageUrl = 'https://cloudzeta.solutions/tools/kubernetes-qos-calculator/';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'FAQPage',
      '@id': `${pageUrl}#faq`,
      mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: f.q,
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
      })),
    },
    {
      '@type': 'WebApplication',
      '@id': `${pageUrl}#app`,
      name: "Cloudzeta Kubernetes QoS Calculator",
      url: pageUrl,
      applicationCategory: 'DeveloperApplication',
      operatingSystem: 'Any modern web browser',
      description: "Free Kubernetes QoS calculator. Determine whether a pod is Guaranteed, Burstable or BestEffort from its requests and limits, with practical eviction and overcommit warnings. No ads, no tracking.",
      offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
      featureList: ["Guaranteed, Burstable and BestEffort classification per the kubelet rules", "Multi-container pods supported", "Warnings for missing limits and overcommit patterns", "Runs offline. Nothing is sent to a server"],
      publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Cloudzeta Solutions', url: 'https://cloudzeta.solutions' },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      itemListElement: [
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://cloudzeta.solutions/' },
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Tools', item: 'https://cloudzeta.solutions/tools/' },
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: "Kubernetes QoS Calculator", item: pageUrl },
      ],
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title="Kubernetes QoS Classes Explained | Guaranteed, Burstable, BestEffort"
  description="What a Kubernetes QoS class is, how the kubelet decides between Guaranteed, Burstable and BestEffort, and what it means for eviction order. Enter your requests and limits to see which you get. Free, no ads."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} slot="head" />

  <Navbar minimal client:load />

  <main>
    <section class="pt-20 pb-12 md:pt-28 md:pb-16 px-4 sm:px-6 bg-[radial-gradient(78%_120%_at_50%_-8%,#eef5ff_0%,#ffffff_58%)]">
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto text-center mb-6 md:mb-7">
        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl md:text-5xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-tx-primary leading-[1.08]">
          Kubernetes QoS Calculator
        </h1>
      </div>

      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <K8sQosCalculator client:load />
        <p class="text-base text-tx-secondary text-center text-balance mt-6">
          Requests and limits in, QoS class and eviction consequences out.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!--
      The queries reaching this page are questions rather than tool searches:
      "what is qos class in kubernetes", "k8s qos classes". The calculator
      answers none of them on its own, so the explanation lives here in plain
      HTML.
    -->
    <section class="py-16 px-6 border-t border-glass-border">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-10">
        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-tx-primary mb-3">
            What a Kubernetes QoS class is
          </h2>
          <p class="text-tx-secondary leading-relaxed">
            A label the kubelet attaches to every pod when it is admitted, derived entirely from the
            requests and limits its containers declare. There is no field to set it in: you get the
            class your resource spec earns. Its purpose is to rank pods against each other for
            eviction, so when a node runs out of memory the class decides who is killed first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-tx-primary mb-3">
            Guaranteed, Burstable and BestEffort
          </h2>
          <p class="text-tx-secondary leading-relaxed mb-3">
            Three rules, checked in order:
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-tx-secondary leading-relaxed mb-3">
            <li>
              <strong class="text-tx-primary">Guaranteed</strong>: every container sets CPU and
              memory requests and limits, and each request equals its limit.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-tx-primary">BestEffort</strong>: no container sets any request or
              limit at all.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-tx-primary">Burstable</strong>: anything else.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-tx-secondary leading-relaxed">
            The rule that catches people is how unforgiving Guaranteed is. One container in a pod
            missing one value, or a request set a little below its limit, drops the entire pod to
            Burstable. Sidecars are the usual culprit: the application container is specified
            carefully and the log shipper beside it is not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-tx-primary mb-3">What the class actually changes</h2>
          <p class="text-tx-secondary leading-relaxed mb-3">
            Eviction order under memory pressure. BestEffort pods are removed first, then Burstable
            pods that have grown past their requests, and Guaranteed pods last. For a stateful or
            latency-critical service, the class is effectively its seat on the lifeboat.
          </p>
          <p class="text-tx-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Worth separating from a different mechanism people conflate with it: exceeding a memory
            limit gets a container OOM-killed by the kernel immediately, whatever its class.
            Exceeding a CPU limit only throttles it. That asymmetry is why a missing memory limit is
            worth flagging, since without one a leak grows until the node itself is in trouble and
            the eviction order decides who pays for it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-tx-primary mb-3">Should everything be Guaranteed?</h2>
          <p class="text-tx-secondary leading-relaxed">
            No. Guaranteed reserves the full limit for every pod, which gives up the overcommit that
            makes a cluster affordable. The usual shape is Guaranteed for critical stateful
            services, Burstable with honest requests for the bulk of workloads, and BestEffort only
            for jobs you would not mind losing. Setting requests you know to be inflated in order to
            reach Guaranteed just moves the waste somewhere harder to see.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section class="py-24 px-6 bg-surface border-t border-glass-border">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold text-tx-primary mb-12 text-center">
          Kubernetes QoS FAQs
        </h2>
        <div class="space-y-3">
          {
            faqs.map((faq) => (
              <details class="group bg-white border border-glass-border rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm">
                <summary class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4 cursor-pointer p-6 font-semibold text-tx-primary list-none hover:text-accent transition-colors">
                  {faq.q}
                  <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-tx-secondary transition-transform group-open:rotate-45" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M12 4v16m8-8H4" />
                  </svg>
                </summary>
                <p class="px-6 pb-6 text-tx-secondary leading-relaxed">{faq.a}</p>
              </details>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <footer class="py-12 px-6 border-t border-glass-border">
      <p class="max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center text-sm text-tx-secondary leading-relaxed">
        A free tool from <a href="/" class="text-tx-primary hover:text-accent font-medium">Cloudzeta
        Solutions</a>. No ads, no tracking, no account. It stays that way.
        <br class="hidden sm:block" />
        See all <a href="/tools/" class="text-tx-primary hover:text-accent font-medium">free tools</a>.
      </p>
    </footer>
  </main>
</Layout>
